[{:db/id "5a7ef8e8-03eb-5e71-9954-b61f0cd0045d" :answer/id #uuid "5a7ef8e8-03eb-5e71-9954-b61f0cd0045d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "aab019ae-93c7-50b3-9811-d455898d1942" :answer/id #uuid "aab019ae-93c7-50b3-9811-d455898d1942" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "996ebf57-fd88-5775-876c-167c46de26ee" :answer/id #uuid "996ebf57-fd88-5775-876c-167c46de26ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "591a446f-82dd-564c-b15f-8e8267e36fdc" :answer/id #uuid "591a446f-82dd-564c-b15f-8e8267e36fdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "cc2ac1da-5902-5d50-a8d4-5ac0daceec12" :answer/id #uuid "cc2ac1da-5902-5d50-a8d4-5ac0daceec12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "779da836-a9bf-5772-a9db-115da484b79e" :answer-field/id #uuid "779da836-a9bf-5772-a9db-115da484b79e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["5a7ef8e8-03eb-5e71-9954-b61f0cd0045d" "aab019ae-93c7-50b3-9811-d455898d1942" "996ebf57-fd88-5775-876c-167c46de26ee" "591a446f-82dd-564c-b15f-8e8267e36fdc" "cc2ac1da-5902-5d50-a8d4-5ac0daceec12"] :answer-field/correct "996ebf57-fd88-5775-876c-167c46de26ee" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "391564c7-a751-5991-b788-dcbb08b27c2c" :question/id #uuid "391564c7-a751-5991-b788-dcbb08b27c2c" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x^{2} + 4 y, \\; 2 x^{2} + 4 x y \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9168d2ed7eeaa5f1dd331a0a986ac7ecfd6b964217a2cb614deb0cdd54437d65.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 y$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 x y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=4 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=4 x.\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds&=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\left(8 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[8 x y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{2}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(8 x^{3}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 x^{4}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["779da836-a9bf-5772-a9db-115da484b79e"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "30eb9b0a-3b5c-550c-97df-45c92636de58" :answer/id #uuid "30eb9b0a-3b5c-550c-97df-45c92636de58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "75dd0d96-1827-5799-ba11-bbee875c6ba2" :answer/id #uuid "75dd0d96-1827-5799-ba11-bbee875c6ba2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "84167764-9866-593d-9f3f-728b74e99429" :answer/id #uuid "84167764-9866-593d-9f3f-728b74e99429" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "193c7f3f-1b13-52cf-b99e-2cb82e2ec402" :answer/id #uuid "193c7f3f-1b13-52cf-b99e-2cb82e2ec402" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "d900e075-bbea-5147-aeda-64c204455c22" :answer/id #uuid "d900e075-bbea-5147-aeda-64c204455c22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "05ec21ed-67a9-5f57-8257-9837d31fa900" :answer-field/id #uuid "05ec21ed-67a9-5f57-8257-9837d31fa900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["30eb9b0a-3b5c-550c-97df-45c92636de58" "75dd0d96-1827-5799-ba11-bbee875c6ba2" "84167764-9866-593d-9f3f-728b74e99429" "193c7f3f-1b13-52cf-b99e-2cb82e2ec402" "d900e075-bbea-5147-aeda-64c204455c22"] :answer-field/correct "84167764-9866-593d-9f3f-728b74e99429" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "9f300428-dd28-5d22-81b0-c450650df73d" :question/id #uuid "9f300428-dd28-5d22-81b0-c450650df73d" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 28 x y, \\; 12 x^{2} y + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f6a5cf9b37fe16f17fe74face946d85b426badf8db710ca4a7ea308a1ff2ec.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=28 x y$ and $Q(x,y)=12 x^{2} y + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=28 y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=12 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds&=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 28 y\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{3}}\\left(12 x^{2} + 28 y\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[12 x^{2} y + 14 y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{3}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(x^{5} \\left(14 x + 12\\right)\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 x^{7} + 2 x^{6}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["05ec21ed-67a9-5f57-8257-9837d31fa900"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "generator" :question-generator/id #uuid "ae8aeed1-6e4f-5d6d-bd4f-c30d442a97bc" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186172099.py"} [:db/add 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/question-generator "generator"] {:db/id 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/questions ["391564c7-a751-5991-b788-dcbb08b27c2c" "9f300428-dd28-5d22-81b0-c450650df73d"]} [:db/ensure 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/validate]]
